<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul Johann Anselm von Feuerbach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Anselm von Feuerbach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul Johann Anselm von Feuerbach was a German jurist and a founder of modern criminal law. He drafted the Bavarian Criminal Code of 1813, which put into law his principle that there can be no crime or punishment without a prior law (nullum crimen, nulla poena sine lege) and abolished torture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It became a model for criminal codes across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and beyond. He was the father of the philosopher Ludwig Feuerbach.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-johann-anselm-von-feuerbach.jpg" alt="Paul Johann Anselm von Feuerbach" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anselm_von_Feuerbach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paul Johann Anselm Ritter von Feuerbach was born on 14 November 1775 in Hainichen, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, and was educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main, where his family moved soon after his birth. At sixteen he ran away from home and went to Jena, where relations helped him to study at the university. He took a doctorate in philosophy in 1795 and married in the same year. The need to earn money turned him from philosophy and history to law, which he disliked but which offered faster advancement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At 23 he became prominent through a vigorous criticism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hobbes">Thomas Hobbes</a>'s theory of civil power. In lectures on criminal law he argued that judges should be strictly limited by the penal code, a doctrine that gave rise to a party called the Rigorists. He is the originator of the maxim nullum crimen, nulla poena sine praevia lege poenali: there is no crime, and so no punishment, if no penal law existed at the time. In 1805 King Maximilian Joseph commanded him to draft a penal code for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> with a high post in the Ministry of Justice, where he was ennobled in 1808.</p><p>Reform of Bavarian penal law began under his influence in 1806 with the abolition of torture. The code he framed was promulgated in 1813 and became the basis for new codes in Württemberg and Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, was adopted in its entirety in the Grand Duchy of Oldenburg, was translated into Swedish by order of the king, and guided the reform of several Swiss cantons' codes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Merkwürdige Criminalfälle (1808 to 1811) and the enlarged Aktenmäßige Darstellung merkwürdiger Verbrechen (1828/29) drew on his cases in the Ministry of Justice, intended to establish a modern criminal psychology. Gerold Schmidt found that they record true events and real people, making them a source for Bavarian local and social history. In 1811 Feuerbach declared against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a>, though his view was later somewhat modified.</p><p>After a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> provinces in 1821 he pleaded for publicity in all legal proceedings. In 1832 he published the first critical summary of the facts about the foundling Kaspar Hauser. He died on 29 May 1833 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>; he and his family believed he had been poisoned because of his work on Hauser, but the cause of death remains unclear.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul Johann Anselm Ritter von Feuerbach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He created a professional civil service, divided the kingdom into administrative districts named after rivers, introduced religious toleration and compulsory schooling, made smallpox vaccination compulsory in 1807 and commissioned <b>Anselm von Feuerbach</b>'s criminal code of 1813.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hainichen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51,11.62&amp;year=1812" aria-label="Hainichen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Paul Johann Anselm von Feuerbach"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5621 5712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5621 5712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51,11.62&amp;year=1812">What was here in 1812? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
